// A borrowing iterator is a VIEW, so it may not be stored in a field. This is the laundering case SPEC
// used to record as a known limitation: a `View<T>` may not be a field, but its iterator — holding the
// same borrowed pointer — could be, and every collection iterator had the same shape.
//
// The repro this replaces was not theoretical. `MapValueIter` is exported, so no qualified-spelling trick
// was needed; the program below compiled clean, `kama check` said OK, and running it read a DEAD STACK
// FRAME (`leak`'s, where the `Map` lived). AddressSanitizer:
//
//     ERROR: AddressSanitizer: stack-buffer-overflow ... READ of size 4
//       #0 std__collections__MapValueIter_int32__next
//
// The `modsp` modification counter is NOT what catches this — it is a pointer into the same dead frame,
// so reading it *is* the use-after-free. It happened to fail fast here; after the storage is reused it
// reads back a matching count and the program proceeds on freed memory.
import { std::collections::Map, std::collections::MapValueIter };

type value Keeper {
    public MapValueIter<int32> it;
    public ctor make(MapValueIter<int32> it) { this.it = it; }
}

fn Keeper leak() {
    Map<int32, int32> m = Map.withCapacity(capacity: 8);
    m.put(key: 1, value: 111);
    return Keeper.make(it: m.values());   // `m` dies here; the iterator keeps its pointer
}

fn int32 main() {
    Keeper k = leak();
    int32 sum = 0;
    foreach (int32 v in k.it) { sum = sum + v; }
    return sum;
}
